# Shell scripts are checked out with LF everywhere, including on Windows.
#
# core.autocrlf is true on the development machine, so without this the CI smoke
# script gets CRLF in the working tree. Git normalises it back on commit, so the
# repository stays correct and the defect stays invisible -- right up until a copy
# with CRLF reaches a Linux runner, where bash reads the carriage return as part of
# the command and answers `$'\r': command not found` on the first line. That failure
# names nothing that would lead anyone to line endings.
#
# Deliberately narrow. A broad set of rules here would renormalise files this commit
# has no business touching.
*.sh text eol=lf
